ipconfig
What it does: Displays the basic network configuration for all network adapters (Ethernet, Wi-Fi, etc.), including your current IPv4 address, Subnet Mask, and Default Gateway.

ipconfig /all
What it does: Displays the full detailed configuration for all adapters. This includes physical (MAC) addresses, DHCP server details, DNS server addresses, and whether DHCP is enabled.

ipconfig /release
What it does: Sends a DHCP release message to your router to drop your current IPv4 address, temporarily disconnecting your computer from the local network.

ipconfig /renew
What it does: Requests a new IPv4 address from the DHCP server (router). This is typically used right after running /release to refresh your IP address.

ipconfig /release6
What it does: Same as /release, but specifically targets and drops your IPv6 address.

ipconfig /renew6
What it does: Requests a new IPv6 address from the DHCP server.

ipconfig /flushdns
What it does: Clears and resets the contents of the DNS Resolver cache. Use this if you are having trouble loading certain websites due to outdated or corrupted DNS records.

